[[Spider-Man]] is the superhero persona of high school student Peter Parker, a character created by [[Marvel Comics]] writer-editor [[Stan Lee]] and [[Steve Ditko]] in 1962 to entice teenage readers.{{Sfn|DeFalco|Lee|2001|p=9, 38}} In the 1970s, Marvel partnered with [[CBS]] to produce a TV adaptation of the character.{{sfn|Burnham|2016|p=133}} The Marvel–CBS partnership yielded three Spider-Man TV films starring [[Nicholas Hammond]] for the live-action program ''[[The Amazing Spider-Man (TV series)|The Amazing Spider-Man]]'', which aired sporadically for thirteen episodes.{{sfn|Rhoades|2008a|p=284}} The first film, ''[[Spider-Man (1977 film)|Spider-Man]]'' (1977), follows the character's quest to thwart an extortionist's plot to kill a group of civilians with a mind control device.{{sfn|Terrace|2024|p=573}} Two sequels, ''[[Spider-Man Strikes Back]]'' (1979) and ''[[Spider-Man: The Dragon's Challenge]]'' (1981), were [[composite film|composites]] of individual episodes of ''The Amazing Spider-Man''.{{sfnm|1a1=Smith|1y=2013|1p=695|2a1=Saffel|2y=2007|2pp=109–111}} Each film received minor distribution in theaters outside of the United States.{{sfn|George|2023|p=59}}

A Japanese Spider-Man adaptation was conceived in the same period. Part of a four-year licensing deal with Marvel,{{sfn|Rhoades|2008a|p=284}} [[Toei Company]] developed a theatrical film, ''[[Spider-Man (1978 film)|Spider-Man]]'' (1978), as a spinoff to the live-action [[tokusatsu]] series of the same name, commonly referred to by its Japanese pronunciation ''[[Spider-Man (Japanese TV series)|Supaidāman]]''.{{sfn|Glaser|2022|pp=28–29}}<ref>{{Cite magazine |last=Abrams |first=Simon |date=December 16, 2018 |title=‘Supaidaman’: Looking Back at the Delightfully Bizarre Japanese Spider-Man |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/supaidaman-should-have-been-spider-man-spider-verse-1169652/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250826211241/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/supaidaman-should-have-been-spider-man-spider-verse-1169652/ |archive-date=August 26, 2025 |access-date=January 25, 2026 |magazine=[[The Hollywood Reporter]]}}</ref> The film's origin story is a significant departure from the source material, portraying Spider-Man as the alter ego of a motorcyclist seeking to avenge his deceased father with powers inherited from Garia, the lone survivor of the extinct Spider Planet.{{sfnm|1a1=Rhoades|1y=2008a|1p=284|2a1=Glaser|2y=2022|2pp=28–29}}

===Feature film development===

After the success of ''[[Superman (1978 film)|Superman]]'' (1978), Marvel began a concerted effort to produce [[feature film]] projects.{{snf|Booker|2014|p=}} They abandoned several early Spider-Man proposals, including a musical and another concept culminating in a battle against [[Nazism|Nazis]] and a 100-foot robot.{{sfn|Raphael|Spurgeon|2004|p=196}} By 1982, [[Roger Corman]], an experienced producer of low-budget [[B films]], [[option (filmmaking)|optioned]] the rights to develop an adaptation with [[Orion Pictures]] from Lee's [[film treatment]].{{sfn|Raphael|Spurgeon|2004|p=196}}<ref>{{Cite news |last=Sagers |first=Aaron |date=August 7, 2015 |title='We Made a Good Little Film': Roger Corman's Oral History of His Fantastic Four |url=https://www.syfy.com/syfywire/we-made-good-little-film-roger-cormans-oral-history-his-fantastic-four |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210206140658/https://www.syfy.com/syfywire/we-made-good-little-film-roger-cormans-oral-history-his-fantastic-four |archive-date=February 6, 2021 |access-date=December 20, 2020 |work=[[Syfy Wire]]}}</ref> Lee's original treatment featured [[Doctor Octopus]] as the primary antagonist and a subplot of nuclear war with the [[Soviet Union]].{{sfn|Raphael|Spurgeon|2004|p=196}} However, Lee and Corman ended their collaboration when they could not agree on a budget.<ref>{{harvnb|O'Connell|2022|p=31}}</ref>

[[The Cannon Group]] executives [[Menahem Golan]] and [[Yoram Globus]] acquired the film rights to Spider-Man for $225,000 in 1985, with a provision that would restore ownership to Marvel should a film not be made within five years.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Grover |first=Ronald |date=April 15, 2002 |title=Unraveling Spider-Man's Tangled Web |work=[[Business Week]] |url=http://www.businessweek.com/bwdaily/dnflash/apr2002/nf20020415_7441.htm |access-date=January 22, 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090510064647/http://www.businessweek.com/bwdaily/dnflash/apr2002/nf20020415_7441.htm |archive-date=May 10, 2009}}</ref>{{sfn|Rhoades|2008b|pp=183–184}} They brought on [[Tobe Hooper]] to outline an origin story with screenwriter [[Leslie Stevens]], the result being one depicting Spider-Man as a literal spider.<ref name=literalspider/> Lee, upset with the changes, persuaded Cannon to discard the work and begin anew.<ref name=literalspider/> [[Joseph Zito]] replaced Hooper as director, and a script was concocted from a pitch developed by Ted Newsom and [[John Brancato and Michael Ferris|John Brancato]].<ref name=literalspider>{{Cite web |last=Ashurst |first=Sam |date=December 31, 2019 |title=The lost Spider-Man horror movie that could have destroyed the MCU |url=https://www.digitalspy.com/movies/a27913075/spider-man-horror-mcu/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250917141844/https://www.digitalspy.com/movies/a27913075/spider-man-horror-mcu/ |archive-date=September 17, 2025 |access-date=September 17, 2025 |website=[[Digital Spy]]}}</ref> In total, Cannon spent $2 million on a script and subsequent rewrites, but financial woes and disputes over the artistic direction frustrated the development.{{sfnm|1a1=Tucker|1y=2017|1p=223|2a1=Wasko|2y=2003|2p=163}} 

After [[Pathé#Natan to Parretti|Pathé Communications]] purchased Cannon, Golan and Globus split, and the Spider-Man film rights transferred to Golan's [[21st Century Film Corporation]] through a [[severance package]], before being sold to [[Carolco Pictures]] for $5 million in 1988.{{sfnm|1a1=Rhoades|1y=2008b|1pp=183–184|2a1=Wasko|2y=2003|2p=163}} Carolco hired [[James Cameron]] as director and screenwriter with a stipulation that granted him authority over the distribution of producing credits, similar to his contract for ''[[Terminator 2: Judgment Day]]'' (1991), another Carolco production.{{sfn|Robinson|Gonzales|Edwards|2023}} Cameron's [[scriptment]], which was about 57 pages long, concentrated on Spider-Man as a morally ambiguous character.<ref name="Hiltzik"/> In the meantime, Carolco extended Cannon's original deal with Marvel to May 1996,<ref name="legal">{{cite news|first=Janet|last=Shprintz|url=https://variety.com/1998/film/news/spider-man-s-legal-web-may-finally-be-unraveled-1117479641/|title=Spider-Man's legal web may finally be unraveled|work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|date=August 19, 1998|access-date=April 21, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250815042903/https://variety.com/1998/film/news/spider-man-s-legal-web-may-finally-be-unraveled-1117479641/|archive-date=August 15, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> but the total cost of production rose to $50 million from its initial $15 million budget, leading the company to abandon the project by 1992.{{sfnm|1a1=Rhoades|1y=2008b|1pp=183–184|2a1=Wasko|2y=2003|2p=163}}

Protracted litigation ensued over the disposition of the Spider-Man copyrights, with Golan suing Carolco in 1993 over the claim that it had violated his contractually guaranteed credit as producer in the Cannon–Marvel agreement.<ref name="legal"/> Carolco then sued [[Viacom (1952–2005)|Viacom]] and [[Sony Pictures|Sony]] subsidiary [[Columbia Pictures]] over the [[broadcast syndication|syndication]] and home video rights, which Golan had sold them in separate sales, and the two studios countersued in lawsuits that, additionally, disputed Marvel's ownership stakes.<ref name="unraveling" />{{sfn|Lee|Mair|2002|pp=236-237}} [[20th Century Fox]], though not a main party in the litigation, contested Cameron's participation with a claim of exclusivity on his services as a director.<ref name="Hiltzik">{{cite news| first=Michael A. |last=Hiltzik |url= https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2002-mar-24-tm-34460-story.html |title=Untangling the Web |work= [[Los Angeles Times]] |date=March 24, 2002 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210614041710/https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2002-mar-24-tm-34460-story.html |archive-date= June 14, 2021|access-date=April 21, 2022}}</ref> By the mid-1990s, Carolco, 21st Century, and Marvel filed for bankruptcy, complicating the dispute.{{sfn|Wasko|2003|p=163}} [[Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer]] (MGM) purchased all 21st Century-owned assets and Carolco's film rights during the proceedings,{{sfn|Rhoades|2008b|pp=183–184}}<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.secinfo.com/$/Search.asp?Find=1-09264|title=Securities and Exchange Commission Document 1-09264|access-date=November 5, 2007|author=Securities and Exchange Commission|date=April 15, 1995|publisher=[[Securities and Exchange Commission]] (requires registration)}}</ref> and instigated another lawsuit alleging fraud in the Cannon–Marvel deal.<ref name="legal"/> According to a 2002 report from the ''[[Los Angeles Times]]'', legal inquiries found that Marvel's licensing agreements overlapped, sometimes on dubious terms.<ref name="Hiltzik"/>

After Marvel reemerged from bankruptcy in 1998, the courts ruled that the rights sold to Golan had expired, reverting the rights to the company.<ref name="unraveling">{{cite news|first=Ronald|last=Grover|date=April 15, 2002|title=Unraveling Spider-Man's Tangled Web|work=[[Bloomberg News]]|url=https://www.bloomberg.com/news/articles/2002-04-14/unraveling-spider-mans-tangled-web|access-date=April 21, 2022|archive-date=May 10, 2009|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090510064647/http://www.businessweek.com/bwdaily/dnflash/apr2002/nf20020415_7441.htm}}</ref> The studios spent the following year settling the remaining lawsuits, owing in part to soaring attorney fees.{{sfn|Lee|Mair|2002|pp=236-237}} Marvel settled with Sony in a merchandising joint venture that conveyed the rights to produce film, television, and sequels to Sony and subsidiary Columbia for $7 million.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1999-mar-02-fi-13115-story.html|first=Michael A.|last=Hiltzik|title=Studio Rights to Spider-Man Are Untangled|date=March 2, 1999|access-date=April 21, 2022|work=[[Los Angeles Times]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250815202916/https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-1999-mar-02-fi-13115-story.html|archive-date=August 15, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |last=Pallotta |first=Frank |date=October 8, 2014|title=Here's why Sony, and not Marvel Studios, owns the rights to Spider-Man |url=https://money.cnn.com/2014/10/08/media/spiderman-rights-marvel-sony/index.html |access-date=January 8, 2026|publisher=[[CNN]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141009153017/https://money.cnn.com/2014/10/08/media/spiderman-rights-marvel-sony/index.html|archive-date=October 9, 2014|url-status=dead}}</ref> At the same time, MGM compromised with Sony by surrendering its claim to the Spider-Man film rights in exchange for rights to ''[[Casino Royale (2006 film)|Casino Royale]]'' (2006) and the global distribution rights to all subsequent [[List of James Bond films|''James Bond'' films]].<ref>{{Cite news |title=Sony Pictures, in an accord with MGM, drops its plan to produce new James Bond movies |work=[[The New York Times]] |date=March 30, 1999 |url=https://www.nytimes.com/1999/03/30/business/media-business-advertising-sony-pictures-accord-with-mgm-drops-its-plan-produce.html |access-date=August 15, 2025 |url-access=subscription |first=James |last=Sterngold |archive-date=27 March 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120327072822/http://www.nytimes.com/1999/03/30/business/media-business-advertising-sony-pictures-accord-with-mgm-drops-its-plan-produce.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Sony's ownership is perpetual provided that they release a new Spider-Man film at least once every five years.<ref>{{Cite news|url=https://www.businessinsider.com/sonys-spider-man-deal-with-disney-and-marvel-studios-explained-2019-8|title=Spider-Man's movie adventures have been a headache for Sony for over a decade, but the character is too valuable to compromise on|last=Clark|first=Travis|work=[[Business Insider]]|access-date=September 27, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250825184010/https://www.businessinsider.com/sonys-spider-man-deal-with-disney-and-marvel-studios-explained-2019-8|archive-date=August 25, 2025|url-status=live|date=21 August 2019}}</ref><!-- ATTENTION! PLEASE READ BEFORE EDITING! Please help keep this section brief. Every detail about the plot of the Spider-Man films does not need to appear here. -->

==Sam Raimi trilogy==
{{See also|Peter Parker (2002 film series character)}}

===''Spider-Man'' (2002)===
{{Main|Spider-Man (2002 film)|l1 = ''Spider-Man'' (2002 film)}}
[[File:Tobey Maguire 2014.jpg|thumb|upright|alt=Cropped photo of Tobey Maguire at the 2014 Toronto Film Festival|[[Tobey Maguire]] in 2014|left]]
Columbia began developing ''Spider-Man'' after their rights acquisition in 1999.<ref name="unraveling" /> [[Sam Raimi]] was not an immediate favorite to direct the film, but producers appointed him director because they were impressed with his enthusiasm.{{sfn|Muir|2004|pp=268–269}} Screenwriter [[David Koepp]] was hired to write the ''Spider-Man'' script, expanding on ideas introduced in Cameron's treatment.<ref name="Hiltzik"/> The script was revised under [[Scott Rosenberg]] and again by [[Alvin Sargent]] to improve dialogue.<ref name="greg">{{cite web |author-link=Greg Dean Schmitz|last=Schmitz|first=Greg |title=Greg's Preview – Spider-Man |url=https://movies.yahoo.com/shop?d=hp&cf=prev&id=1808406011&gpt=ch |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070429050554/http://movies.yahoo.com/shop?d=hp&cf=prev&id=1808406011&gpt=ch |archive-date=April 29, 2007 |access-date=August 18, 2025 |publisher=[[Yahoo!]]}}</ref>{{sfn|Gross|2002|pp=206–208}} Columbia signed [[Tobey Maguire]] to a three-picture, $3–4 million [[pay-or-play contract]] as [[Peter Parker (2002 film series character)|Peter Parker]] in July 2000, which stipulated greater compensation for subsequent films.<ref name="MaguireCast">{{cite news|first1=Michael|last1=Fleming|first2=Claude|last2=Brodesser|url=https://variety.com/2000/film/news/maguire-spins-spider-man-1117784384/|title=Maguire spins 'Spider-Man'|work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|date=July 31, 2000|access-date=August 18, 2025|archive-date=October 12, 2007|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071012002038/http://variety.com/article/VR1117784384.html?categoryid=13&cs=1&query=spider-man|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="spins">{{Cite magazine |last1=Michael Fleming |last2=Claude Brodesser |date=July 31, 2000 |title=Maguire spins 'Spider-Man' |url=https://variety.com/2000/film/news/maguire-spins-spider-man-1117784384/ |magazine=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071012002038/https://variety.com/article/VR1117784384.html?categoryid=13&cs=1&query=spider-man |archive-date=October 12, 2007 |url-status=live |access-date=August 18, 2025}}</ref> Maguire was Raimi's preferred choice based on his performance in ''[[The Cider House Rules]]'' (1999).{{sfn|Muir|2004|p=270}} [[Leonardo DiCaprio]], [[Freddie Prinze Jr.]], [[Heath Ledger]], [[Scott Speedman]] and [[Wes Bentley]] were among the actors Columbia considered to star.<ref>{{cite news | url=https://www.theguardian.com/film/2000/aug/01/news1| title=Columbia name their Spiderman | newspaper=[[The Guardian]] | date=August 1, 2000|access-date=August 22, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250822174323/https://www.theguardian.com/film/2000/aug/01/news1|archive-date=August 22, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>{{sfn|Pine|2023|p=}} 

''Spider-Man'' explores Peter's struggles adapting to his newfound [[superpower (ability)|superpowers]], which he acquires from the bite of a genetically engineered spider. After the death of his uncle [[Uncle Ben|Ben]] ([[Cliff Robertson]]), Peter vows to contain crime in New York, climaxing in a confrontation with the [[Norman Osborn (2002 film series character)|Green Goblin]] ([[Willem Dafoe]]). ''Spider-Man'' was shot from January to June 2001,<ref name="greg" /> and released in May 2002 after Sony extended the post-production schedule.<ref>{{cite web |date=September 14, 2000 |title=''Spider-Man'' Crawls into 2002 |url=http://movies.ign.com/articles/034/034459p1.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120820015235/http://movies.ign.com/articles/034/034459p1.html |archive-date=August 20, 2012 |access-date=August 18, 2025 |website=[[IGN]]}}</ref> It emerged as the [[2002 in film|third highest-grossing film of 2002]] with $825 million globally, and was nominated for [[Academy Award for Best Visual Effects|Best Visual Effects]] and [[Academy Award for Best Sound Mixing|Best Sound]] at the [[75th Academy Awards]].<ref>{{cite news|first=Marc|last=Malkin|url=https://variety.com/2023/film/columns/amy-pascal-spider-man-first-movie-1235644652/|title='Spider-man' players caught in sequel web|work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|date=June 14, 2023|access-date=August 18, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230615180058/https://variety.com/2023/film/columns/amy-pascal-spider-man-first-movie-1235644652/|archive-date=June 15, 2023|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Oscars2003">{{cite web |title=The 75th Academy Awards (2003) Nominees and Winners |url=http://www.oscars.org/awards/academyawards/legacy/ceremony/75th-winners.html |access-date=August 18, 2025 |publisher=[[Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences]] |archive-date=October 1, 2014 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20141001074247/https://www.oscars.org/awards/academyawards/legacy/ceremony/75th-winners.html |url-status=live}}</ref> ''Spider-Man'' was a catalyst in the rise of [[superhero film]] and greatly influenced Hollywood conception of superheroes.<ref>{{cite news|first=Adam|last=Vary|url=https://variety.com/2022/film/features/spider-man-2002-oral-history-sam-raimi-sony-pictures-1235240553/|title='Spider-Man' at 20: How Sam Raimi and Sony Pictures Rescued the Superhero Genre and Changed Hollywood Forever|work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|date=April 27, 2022|access-date=January 25, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20260103151509/https://variety.com/2022/film/features/spider-man-2002-oral-history-sam-raimi-sony-pictures-1235240553/|archive-date=January 3, 2026|url-status=live}}</ref>

===''Spider-Man 2'' (2004)===
{{Main|Spider-Man 2|l1 = ''Spider-Man 2''}}
A sequel to ''Spider-Man'' was announced by Columbia in April 2002. The studio re-hired most of the filmmaking crew responsible for the creation of the original film.<ref name=sequelapril2002>{{cite news|first=Claude|last=Brodesser|url=https://variety.com/2002/film/news/spider-man-players-caught-in-sequel-web-1117864968/|title='Spider-man' players caught in sequel web|work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|date=April 4, 2002|access-date=August 18, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250818203007/https://variety.com/2002/film/news/spider-man-players-caught-in-sequel-web-1117864968/|archive-date=August 18, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> The completed script coalesced from a draft conceived by Koepp, [[Alfred Gough]], and [[Miles Millar]],<ref name="greg"/><ref name="raimi beeb">{{cite news|first=Stella|last=Papamichael|title=Sam Raimi|publisher=[[BBC]]|date=July 9, 2004|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/films/2004/07/09/sam_raimi_spiderman_2_interview.shtml|access-date=April 30, 2007|archive-date=February 21, 2007|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070221124540/http://www.bbc.co.uk/films/2004/07/09/sam_raimi_spiderman_2_interview.shtml|url-status=live}}</ref> with Sargent credited as the lead screenwriter.<ref>{{Cite news |last1=Smith |first1=Harrison |title=Alvin Sargent, Oscar-winning screenwriter of 'Ordinary People' and Spider-Man films, dies at 92 |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/obituaries/alvin-sargent-oscar-winning-screenwriter-of-ordinary-people-and-spider-man-films-dies-at-92/2019/05/12/70e9a28e-74c4-11e9-b7ae-390de4259661_story.html|access-date=January 26, 2026 |newspaper=[[The Washington Post]] |date=May 12, 2019 |archive-date=May 13, 2019 |url-access=subscription|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190513030736/https://www.washingtonpost.com/local/obituaries/alvin-sargent-oscar-winning-screenwriter-of-ordinary-people-and-spider-man-films-dies-at-92/2019/05/12/70e9a28e-74c4-11e9-b7ae-390de4259661_story.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Maguire received an upfront salary of $17 million after months of negotiations for a new contract.<ref name=fired/> The actor complained of persistent pain in his back sustained while shooting ''[[Seabiscuit (film)|Seabiscuit]]'' (2003), and Columbia fired him as they considered his behavior disruptive.<ref name=fired>{{cite news|url=https://www.theguardian.com/arts/fridayreview/story/0,12102,1235533,00.html|title=Spider-Man 2 Budget|website=[[The Guardian]]|date=April 30, 2002|access-date=November 7, 2006|first=Archie|last=Thomas|archive-date=November 19, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20201119180834/https://www.theguardian.com/film/2004/jun/11/3|url-status=live}}</ref> The studio reversed its decision when [[Ronald Meyer]], the then-president of [[Vivendi Universal]] and Maguire's father-in-law, intervened.<ref name=fired/> 

The ''Spider-Man 2'' plot was partially inspired by the "Spider-Man No More!" story arc in ''[[The Amazing Spider-Man]]'' comics.{{sfn|Burke|2015|p=15}} Peter's powers become impotent as a result of strife in his daily life, forcing him to repudiate his duties as Spider-Man. Meanwhile, his mentor [[Otto Octavius (film character)|Dr. Otto Octavius]] ([[Alfred Molina]]) conducts a nuclear experiment that corrupts the tentacled contraption fused to his spine and then his mind, transforming him into Doctor Octopus, a mad scientist committed to creating a fusion reactor to destroy New York. Filming occurred in 2003, and ''Spider-Man 2'' premiered in theaters in June 2004.<ref name="greg"/> The film won [[Academy Award for Best Visual Effects|Best Visual Effects]] at the [[77th Academy Awards]].<ref name="Oscars2005">{{cite web|url=http://www.oscars.org/awards/academyawards/legacy/ceremony/77th-winners.html |title=The 77th Academy Awards (2005) Nominees and Winners |access-date=November 20, 2011 |publisher=[[Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140610213020/http://www.oscars.org/awards/academyawards/legacy/ceremony/77th-winners.html |archive-date=June 10, 2014 }}</ref>

===''Spider-Man 3'' (2007)===
{{Main|Spider-Man 3|l1 = ''Spider-Man 3''}}
Because the studio wanted to commence soon on a sequel, Raimi and his brother [[Ivan Raimi|Ivan]] spent about two months preparing a treatment while ''Spider-Man 2'' ran in theaters.<ref name="web">{{cite magazine |first=Steve|last=Daly |title=World Wide Web |page=3 |magazine=[[Entertainment Weekly]] |date=April 17, 2007 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20070422201143/http://www.ew.com/ew/article/0%2C%2C20035326_3%2C00.html |url=https://ew.com/article/2007/04/26/summer-movie-preview-whats-ahead-spidey/ |access-date=August 21, 2025 |archive-date=April 22, 2007 |url-status=live}}</ref> Sargent returned to write a screenplay following the direction of the Raimi treatment.<ref name="web"/><ref>{{cite magazine | author=Nicole Laporte | url=https://variety.com/2005/film/markets-festivals/sargent-to-spin-more-spidey-tales-1117916239/ | title=Sargent to spin more Spidey tales | magazine=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] | date=January 12, 2005 | access-date=August 21, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250822175352/https://variety.com/2005/film/markets-festivals/sargent-to-spin-more-spidey-tales-1117916239/|archive-date=August 22, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> The filmmakers were interested in resolving the character arcs with a tale of redemption and forgiveness.<ref name="forgiveness">{{cite news | author = Heather Newgen | title = Spider-Man 3 Interviews: Director Sam Raimi | publisher = [[Mandatory (company)|SuperHeroHype]]| date = April 21, 2007 | url = http://www.superherohype.com/features/articles/93443-spider-man-3-interviews-director-sam-raimi | access-date = August 21, 2025 | archive-date = October 21, 2012 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20121021123842/http://www.superherohype.com/features/articles/93443-spider-man-3-interviews-director-sam-raimi | url-status = live }}</ref> They also developed the script with an emphasis on Peter's evolving relationship with [[Mary Jane Watson (2002 film series character)|Mary Jane Watson]] ([[Kirsten Dunst]]).<ref name="web"/> ''Spider-Man 3'' sees Peter facing multiple threats, including [[Flint Marko (Sam Raimi film series)|Flint Marko / Sandman]] ([[Thomas Haden Church]]), an extraterrestrial [[Symbiote (comics)|symbiote]] that nearly consumes him, and [[Eddie Brock|Edward "Eddie" Brock Jr.]] / [[Venom (character)|Venom]] ([[Topher Grace]]). Filming took place from January to July 2006,<ref name="photo">{{cite news | author=Grant Curtis | url=http://www.sonypictures.com/movies/spiderman3/blog/archives/2006/07/welcome_back.php | title=Grant Curtis – Welcome back | publisher=[[Sony Pictures]] | date=July 28, 2006|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20071012191639/http://sonypictures.com/movies/spiderman3/blog/archives/2006/07/welcome_back.php| archive-date=October 12, 2007 | access-date=August 21, 2025}}</ref> and the film was released in May 2007.<ref>{{cite magazine | author=Ian Mohr | url=https://variety.com/2007/film/markets-festivals/spider-man-3-sets-records-2-1117964093/ | title='Spider-Man 3' sets records | magazine=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]] | date=May 1, 2007 | access-date=August 22, 2025 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121106155841/http://www.variety.com/article/VR1117964093?refCatId=13 | archive-date=November 6, 2012 | url-status=live}}</ref> Despite ending the theatrical run grossing $890.9 million,<ref name="BOM">{{cite web |url=https://boxofficemojo.com/movies/?id=spiderman3.htm |title=Spider-Man 3 (2007)|website=[[Box Office Mojo]] |access-date=August 21, 2025 |archive-date=February 1, 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090201023252/http://boxofficemojo.com/movies/?id=spiderman3.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> ''Spider-Man 3'' drew mixed reviews in the media.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.metacritic.com/movie/spider-man-3/|title=Spider-Man 3 (2007)|website=[[Metacritic]]|access-date=August 21, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250912205625/https://www.metacritic.com/movie/spider-man-3/|archive-date=September 12, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Cancellation of ''Spider-Man 4''===
[[File:Spider-Man-Logo.svg|thumb|upright=1.4|alt=Logo of the ''Spider-Man'' franchise|Franchise logo from 2002 to 2007]]
Media coverage of ''Spider-Man 4'' gives contradictory accounts about the project's development. One report disseminated by ''[[Deadline Hollywood]]'' said Sony was prioritizing the sequel to [[back-to-back film production|shoot back-to-back]] with a fifth film.<ref name=furthersequels>{{Cite web |last=Finke |first=Nikki |date=September 5, 2008 |title=EXCLUSIVE: Sony Locks In Sam Raimi & Tobey Maguire in 'Spider-Man 4′; Studio May Shoot '4′ and '5′ At The Same Time|url=https://deadline.com/2008/09/exclusive-sony-sets-sam-raimi-studio-may-shoot-4-and-5-at-same-time-6868/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121106203133/http://www.deadline.com/2008/09/exclusive-sony-sets-sam-raimi-studio-may-shoot-4-and-5-at-same-time/ |archive-date=November 6, 2012 |access-date=August 22, 2025 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref>  However, Raimi maintained in a 2009 interview that the studio pursued only ''Spider-Man 4'' and had no definitive proposals for further sequels.<ref>{{cite news|title=Sam Raimi 'Can't Imagine' Doing 'Spider-Man 4' Without Kirsten Dunst, Only Working On Fourth Film|publisher=[[MTV.com|MTV]]|date=March 15, 2009 |url=http://www.mtv.com/news/2593794/exclusive-sam-raimi-cant-imagine-doing-spider-man-4-without-kirsten-dunst-only-working-on-fourth-film/|access-date=April 21, 2022|archive-date=March 17, 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090317222846/http://splashpage.mtv.com/2009/03/15/exclusive-sam-raimi-cant-imagine-doing-spider-man-4-without-kirsten-dunst-only-working-on-fourth-film/}}</ref> Even so, Sony recruited screenwriter [[James Vanderbilt]] to write scripts for a fourth, fifth, and sixth Spider-Man film in October 2007 and August 2009 after initial reports claimed the studio was negotiating with Koepp.<ref>{{cite news|first=Michael|last=Fleming |url=https://www.variety.com/2007/biz/news/columbia-koepp-talk-spider-man-1117957843/|title=Columbia, Koepp talk 'Spider-Man'|work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|date=January 21, 2007|access-date=April 21, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090513053024/http://www.variety.com/article/VR1117957843.html?categoryid=13&cs=1|archive-date=May 13, 2009}}</ref><ref name="script">{{cite news |url=https://www.variety.com/2009/film/markets-festivals/sony-sets-writer-to-spin-spider-man-1118007333/ |title=Sony sets writer to spin 'Spider-Man'|last=Fleming|first=Michael|date=August 16, 2009|work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|access-date=April 21, 2022|archive-date=June 25, 2010|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100625050426/http://www.variety.com/article/VR1118007333.html?categoryid=13&cs=1}}</ref> 

The ''Spider-Man 4'' script was rewritten by [[David Lindsay-Abaire]] and [[Gary Ross]] in November 2008 and October 2009.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/2595204/exclusive-sam-raimi-hopes-to-start-shooting-spider-man-4-in-march-2010/|title=Sam Raimi Hopes To Start Shooting 'Spider-Man 4' In March 2010|first=Eric|last=Ditzian|date=October 9, 2009|publisher=[[MTV]]|access-date=April 21, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160315221857/http://www.mtv.com/news/2595204/exclusive-sam-raimi-hopes-to-start-shooting-spider-man-4-in-march-2010/|archive-date=March 15, 2016}}</ref> Raimi had been unhappy with ''Spider-Man 3'' and wanted to create a satisfying narrative to conclude the franchise, but found himself at odds with Sony because he was unable to rectify problems in the story within the allotted time.<ref name=vulturedeadline>{{cite news|last=Buchanan|first=Kyle |url=https://www.vulture.com/2013/03/sam-raimi-on-oz-and-two-huge-films-he-never-made.html|title=Sam Raimi on Oz, The Avengers, and Two Huge Movies He Never Made|work=[[Vulture (website)|Vulture]]|date=March 6, 2013|access-date=March 6, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250829041357/https://www.vulture.com/2013/03/sam-raimi-on-oz-and-two-huge-films-he-never-made.html|archive-date=August 29, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> The problems were such that the release was postponed several times to accommodate changes in the writing.<ref name="reboot">{{cite news|last1=Finke|first1=Nikki|last2=Fleming |first2=Mike|url=https://deadline.com/2010/01/urgent-spider-man-4-scrapped-as-is-raimi-and-cast-out-franchise-reboot-planned-21993/|title='Spider-Man 4' Scrapped; Sam Rami & Tobey Maguire & Cast Out; Franchise Reboot for 2012|website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]|date=January 11, 2010|access-date=April 21, 2022 |url-status=live|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220401141857/https://deadline.com/2010/01/urgent-spider-man-4-scrapped-as-is-raimi-and-cast-out-franchise-reboot-planned-21993/|archive-date=April 1, 2022}}</ref> Ultimately, Raimi withdrew as ''Spider-Man 4'' director, prompting Sony to cancel the film in January 2010 and begin development of a reboot.<ref name="reboot"/> The media has since publicized information about plot, characters, and casting from interviews conducted with filmmakers involved in the project.{{efn|Attributed to multiple sources:<ref name=vulturedeadline/><ref>{{cite news|first=Eric|last=Goldman|title=Exclusive: Lizard Leapin' Into Spidey 4?|website=[[IGN]]|date=January 23, 2007 |url=https://www.ign.com/articles/2007/01/23/exclusive-lizard-leapin-into-spidey-4|access-date=April 21, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081230040122/http://uk.movies.ign.com/articles/757/757729p1.html|archive-date=December 30, 2008}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.mtv.com/news/2595268/exclusive-sam-raimi-confirms-bruce-campbell-will-have-meaty-role-in-spider-man-4/|title=Sam Raimi Confirms Bruce Campbell Will Have 'Meaty Role' in 'Spider-Man 4' |first=Eric|last=Ditzian|publisher=[[MTV]]|date=October 19, 2009|access-date=April 21, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170911205009/http://www.mtv.com/news/2595268/exclusive-sam-raimi-confirms-bruce-campbell-will-have-meaty-role-in-spider-man-4/|archive-date=September 11, 2017}}</ref><ref name="SpiderMan4ConceptArt">{{cite web |url=https://gizmodo.com/spider-man-4-storyboards-reveal-iconic-villains-and-hug-1779949984|title=Spider-Man 4 Storyboards Reveal Iconic Villains and Huge Action That Never Was|last=Lussier|first=Germain |work=[[Gizmodo]]|date=June 1, 2016|access-date=April 21, 2022|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250912221551/https://gizmodo.com/spider-man-4-storyboards-reveal-iconic-villains-and-hug-1779949984|archive-date=September 12, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.movieline.com/2009/12/exclusive-spider-man-4-circling-john-malkovich-anne-hathaway.php|last=Buchanan|first=Kyle|date=December 8, 2009|title=EXCLUSIVE: Spider-Man 4 Circling John Malkovich, Anne Hathaway|work=[[Movieline]]|access-date=December 12, 2009 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20091213005246/http://www.movieline.com/2009/12/exclusive-spider-man-4-circling-john-malkovich-anne-hathaway.php?page=all|archive-date=December 13, 2009|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.slashfilm.com/581697/spider-man-4-animatic/|title='Spider-Man 4' Fight Animatic Shows Off An Action Scene From Sam Raimi's Abandoned Sequel|first=Ben|last=Pearson|date=June 14, 2021|work=[[/Film]]|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20241207122524/https://www.slashfilm.com/581697/spider-man-4-animatic/|archive-date=December 7, 2024|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name="Kraven SM4">{{cite magazine|last=Hiatt|first=Brian|url=https://www.rollingstone.com/movies/movie-features/sam-raimi-multiverse-of-madness-doctor-strange-interview-spider-man-1344342/|title=From 'Spider-Man' to 'Doctor Strange': How Sam Raimi Conquered the Superhero Multiverse (Again)|magazine=[[Rolling Stone]]|date=April 30, 2022|access-date=April 30, 2022|archive-url= https://web.archive.org/web/20250912224704/https://www.rollingstone.com/tv-movies/tv-movie-features/sam-raimi-multiverse-of-madness-doctor-strange-interview-spider-man-1344342/|archive-date=September 12, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>}}

==Marc Webb films==
{{See also|Peter Parker (The Amazing Spider-Man film series)|l1=Peter Parker (''The Amazing Spider-Man'' film series)}}

===''The Amazing Spider-Man'' (2012)===
{{Main|The Amazing Spider-Man (film)|l1 = ''The Amazing Spider-Man'' (film)}}

[[File:Andrew Garfield by Gage Skidmore (cropped).jpg|thumb|upright|alt=Photo of Andrew Garfield at the 2013 San Diego Comic-Con|[[Andrew Garfield]] in 2013|right]]

Columbia committed to a Spider-Man reboot with [[Marvel Studios]], envisioning an origin story that would showcase Peter's civilian life.<ref name="Webb">{{cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2010/01/toldja-sony-pictures-to-officially-announce-marc-webb-as-spider-man-reboot-director-22925/|title=TOLDJA! Sony Announces '(500) Days' Marc Webb As 'Spider-Man' Reboot Director|last1=Finke|first1=Nikki|last2=Fleming |first2=Mike|work=[[Deadline Hollywood]]|date=January 19, 2010|access-date=August 25, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250825144548/https://deadline.com/2010/01/toldja-sony-pictures-to-officially-announce-marc-webb-as-spider-man-reboot-director-22925/|archive-date=August 25, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> They hired [[Marc Webb]] as director from a shortlist of filmmakers including [[David Fincher]] and [[Wes Anderson]].<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.mtv.com/news/2597287/spider-man-reboot-david-fincher/|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170731123832/http://www.mtv.com/news/2597287/spider-man-reboot-david-fincher/|url-status=dead|archive-date=July 31, 2017|title = 'Spider-Man' Reboot is 'Risky,' Says David Fincher|publisher = [[MTV]]}}</ref> Sargent, Vanderbilt, and [[Steve Kloves]] produced the finished script.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Columbia Pictures Announces Title of the Next 'Spider-Man' Film |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/columbia-pictures-announces-title-next-99458/ |first=Pamela|last=McClintock|date=February 14, 2011|access-date=January 26, 2026 |work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |archive-date=August 15, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250815150242/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/columbia-pictures-announces-title-next-99458/ |url-status=live }}</ref> Casting reflected specifications for mostly unknown actors.<ref>{{Cite news |last=Kit |first=Borys |date=May 27, 2010 |title=Meet the Men Who Could Play Spider-Man |work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |url=https://www.reuters.com/article/us-spiderman-idUSTRE64Q0Z820100527 |url-status=live |access-date=August 25, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120117010552/http://www.reuters.com/article/2010/05/27/us-spiderman-idUSTRE64Q0Z820100527 |archive-date=January 17, 2012}}</ref> [[Andrew Garfield]] joined as [[Peter Parker (The Amazing Spider-Man film series)|Peter Parker]] in July 2010, signing a three-picture deal with a starting salary of $500,000.<ref name=Garcasting>{{cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2010/07/spider-mans-choice-of-andrew-garfield-latest-in-several-unpredictable-turns-to-reawaken-tired-franchise-51366/|title=New 'Spider-Man' Andrew Garfield Latest Twist To Reboot Tired Franchise|last=Fleming |first=Mike|work=[[Deadline Hollywood]]|date=July 1, 2010|access-date=August 25, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250825153232/https://deadline.com/2010/07/spider-mans-choice-of-andrew-garfield-latest-in-several-unpredictable-turns-to-reawaken-tired-franchise-51366/|archive-date=August 25, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> Webb said he was convinced by Garfield's [[screen test]] of a cutscene eating a burger.<ref>{{Cite web |last=McDaniel, Matt |date=April 16, 2012 |title='The Amazing Spider-Man' director Marc Webb reveals how Andrew Garfield won the role |url=https://movies.yahoo.com/blogs/movie-talk/amazing-spider-man-director-mark-webb-reveals-andrew-181735297.html |access-date=August 25, 2025 |publisher=Yahoo! |archive-date=October 26, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121026172735/http://movies.yahoo.com/blogs/movie-talk/amazing-spider-man-director-mark-webb-reveals-andrew-181735297.html?fb_action_ids=10150688583914423&fb_action_types=news.reads&fb_ref=type%3Aread%2Cuser%3Axe-lCHu4XQbzpqcg9jXkdXlfu9E&fb_source=aggregation&fb_aggregation_id=172808106127348&code=AQCpvKL6FQouiu32uhxW69bXw3sHboZ_6Ul60mBESgqa7Jhbjmeievkkuk-pHHnDWey8o7vH4iW5kKtDC3wN4f5iBStMUl5UJ8X3k9iGE8OZ_cA32UNN6GPzUHO_Ouaea9-PFkue2D42gPHJn0MaKY6M81uodWFLGorRYD2MfrB6qP4NOBWidWIUcWp1vPRHpdQ |url-status=live }}</ref> 

The filmmakers felt it was important Peter's perception as an outcast be adapted to a modern context.<ref name="web shooters 2">{{Cite news |last=Boucher |first=Jeff |date=February 15, 2011 |title='Spider-Man': Andrew Garfield is 'glad that people like the suit'&nbsp;— but do they? |work=[[Los Angeles Times]] |url=http://herocomplex.latimes.com/2011/02/15/spider-man-andrew-garfield-is-glad-that-people-like-the-suit-but-do-they/ |access-date=August 25, 2025 |archive-date=June 27, 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20120627041446/http://herocomplex.latimes.com/2011/02/15/spider-man-andrew-garfield-is-glad-that-people-like-the-suit-but-do-they/ |url-status=live }}</ref> ''The Amazing Spider-Man'' features Peter confronting the [[Lizard (character)|Lizard]], the monstrous form of Curt Connors ([[Rhys Ifans]]), an [[Oscorp]] scientist who previously had a partnership with Peter's deceased father. It was shot from December 2010 to April 2011,<ref name="RED">{{Cite web |date=December 7, 2010 |title=Spider-Man Starts Shooting Using Epic Digital Cameras |url=https://www.superherohype.com/news/111895-spider-man-starts-shooting-using-epic-digital-cameras |access-date=December 31, 2021 |publisher=[[CraveOnline]] |archive-date=April 21, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200421114701/https://www.superherohype.com/news/111895-spider-man-starts-shooting-using-epic-digital-cameras |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="pre-production">{{Cite news |last=Malkin |first=Marc |date=April 26, 2011 |title=''Spider-Man'' Reboot Wrapping Up Filming... Finally |publisher=[[E! Online]] |url=https://www.eonline.com/news/238568/_i_spider_man__i__reboot_wrapping_up_filming___finally |access-date=November 28, 2011 |archive-date=August 17, 2020 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200817212249/https://www.eonline.com/news/238568/_i_Spider_Man__i__Reboot_Wrapping_Up_Filming___Finally |url-status=live }}</ref> and was released in the United States in July 2012.<ref>{{Cite news |last=McClintock |first=Pamela |date=July 3, 2012 |title=Box Office Report: 'Amazing Spider-Man' Headed for Strong $27 Mil-Plus Tuesday |work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/box-office-report-amazing-spider-344625/ |url-status=live |access-date=August 25, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250825225208/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/box-office-report-amazing-spider-344625/ |archive-date=August 25, 2025}}</ref> ''The Amazing Spider-Man'' finished as the [[2012 in film#Highest-grossing films|seventh highest-grossing film of 2012]], amassing $758 million at the box office.<ref>{{Cite web |title=2012 Worldwide Box Office |url=https://www.boxofficemojo.com/year/world/2012/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191206104743/https://www.boxofficemojo.com/year/world/2012/ |archive-date=December 6, 2019 |access-date=August 25, 2025 |website=[[Box Office Mojo]]}}</ref>

===''The Amazing Spider-Man 2'' (2014)===
{{main|The Amazing Spider-Man 2|l1 = ''The Amazing Spider-Man 2''}}

Webb and Garfield were confirmed in the press to be returning for ''The Amazing Spider-Man 2'' in September 2012.<ref name=Webb2/> Vanderbilt was brought back to outline a draft, while scriptwriting responsibilities were assigned to [[Jeff Pinkner]], [[Roberto Orci]] and [[Alex Kurtzman]].<ref name=Webb2>{{cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2012/09/andrew-garfield-and-marc-webb-sign-on-for-spidey-sequels-345024/|title=Andrew Garfield And Marc Webb Sign On For Spidey Sequel Set For May 2014|last=Fleming |first=Mike|work=[[Deadline Hollywood]]|date=September 28, 2012|access-date=August 25, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250825193508/https://deadline.com/2012/09/andrew-garfield-and-marc-webb-sign-on-for-spidey-sequels-345024/|archive-date=August 25, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | url = https://deadline.com/2012/04/alex-kurtzman-roberto-orci-writing-sequel-to-amazing-spider-man-261109/ | title = Alex Kurtzman & Roberto Orci Re-Writing Sequel To 'Amazing Spider-Man' | website=[[Deadline Hollywood]] | date= April 24, 2012 | access-date=August 25, 2025 | last=Fleming | first=Mike | archive-date=October 17, 2013 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20131017180406/http://www.deadline.com/2012/04/alex-kurtzman-roberto-orci-writing-sequel-to-amazing-spider-man/ | url-status=live}}</ref> Filmmakers redesigned the Spider-Man costume to more closely resemble both comic book suits and the suit in the Raimi films.<ref>{{cite web|last1=Phili|first1=Stelios|url=https://www.gq.com/gallery/style-reconnaissance-the-amazing-spider-man-2|title=Style Reconnaissance: The Amazing Spider-Man 2|work=[[GQ (magazine)|GQ]]|url-access=limited|date=May 14, 2014|access-date=January 26, 2026|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230920203408/https://www.gq.com/gallery/style-reconnaissance-the-amazing-spider-man-2|archive-date=September 20, 2023|url-status=live}}</ref> ''The Amazing Spider-Man 2'' depicts Peter's quest to protect [[Gwen Stacy (The Amazing Spider-Man film series)|Gwen Stacy]] ([[Emma Stone]]), resulting in battles with the electricity-manipulating [[Max Dillon (film character)|Electro]] ([[Jamie Foxx]]) and a vengeful [[Harry Osborn (The Amazing Spider-Man film series)|Harry Osborn]] ([[Dane DeHaan]]). Filming lasted about 100 days, followed by the theatrical rollout in May 2014.<ref>{{cite web | url = https://www.vibe.com/news/movies-tv/amazing-spider-man-2-wraps-marc-webb-tweets-celebratory-video-164221/ | title = 'The Amazing Spider-Man 2' Wraps; Marc Webb Tweets Celebratory Video | website=[[Vibe (magazine)|Vibe]] | date= June 26, 2013 | access-date=August 25, 2025 | last=Fleming | first=Mike | archive-date=August 25, 2025 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20250825221923/https://www.vibe.com/news/movies-tv/amazing-spider-man-2-wraps-marc-webb-tweets-celebratory-video-164221/ | url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{Cite news |last=Lee |first=Ashley |date=May 2, 2014 |title='The Amazing Spider-Man 2': What the Critics Are Saying |work=[[The Hollywood Reporter]] |url=https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/amazing-spider-man-2-review-700576/ |url-status=live |access-date=August 25, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250825223734/https://www.hollywoodreporter.com/movies/movie-news/amazing-spider-man-2-review-700576/ |archive-date=August 25, 2025}}</ref> ''The Amazing Spider-Man 2'', while profitable, failed to replicate the success of ''The Amazing Spider-Man''.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2015/03/amazing-spider-man-2-profit-box-office-2014-1201389608/|title=No. 18 'Amazing Spider-Man 2' – 2014 Most Valuable Movie Blockbuster Tournament|last=Fleming |first=Mike|work=[[Deadline Hollywood]]|date=March 10, 2015|access-date=August 25, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250825230205/https://deadline.com/2015/03/amazing-spider-man-2-profit-box-office-2014-1201389608/|archive-date=August 25, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>

===Cancellation===
[[File:The amazing Spider-Man.svg|thumbnail|upright=1.4|alt=Logo of ''The Amazing Spider-Man'' franchise|Franchise logo from 2012 to 2014]]

''The Amazing Spider-Man''{{'s}} success spawned immediate discussion of an expanded Spider-Man film universe.<ref name="UniverseAnnouncement">{{Cite web |last=Patten |first=Dominic |date=December 13, 2013 |title=Sony Sets Spider-Man Spinoffs 'Venom,' 'Sinister Six' With New "Franchise Brain Trust" |url=https://deadline.com/2013/12/spider-man-spinoffs-sony-marc-webb-alex-kurtzman-roberto-orci-jeff-pinkner-ed-solomon-drew-goddard-651848/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170524030650/https://deadline.com/2013/12/spider-man-spinoffs-sony-marc-webb-alex-kurtzman-roberto-orci-jeff-pinkner-ed-solomon-drew-goddard-651848/ |archive-date=May 24, 2017 |access-date=May 20, 2017 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref> Sony commissioned a third and fourth sequel for releases in 2016 and 2018; they secured Webb's commitment as director only for the former.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://deadline.com/2013/06/spider-man-sequels-spider-man-3-spider-man-4-release-dates-522803/|title=Sony Doing Two More 'Spider-Man' Movies, Sets Dates For 2016 And 2018|work=[[Deadline Hollywood]]|date=June 17, 2013|access-date=August 26, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250826045100/https://deadline.com/2013/06/spider-man-sequels-spider-man-3-spider-man-4-release-dates-522803/|archive-date=August 26, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|url=https://variety.com/2014/film/news/analysts-sony-pictures-needs-to-release-more-release-more-tentpoles-1201118909/|title=Analysts: Sony Needs More Bigscreen Franchises|last=Cheney|first=Alexandra|work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|date=February 25, 2014|access-date=February 25, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250912225652/https://variety.com/2014/film/news/analysts-sony-pictures-needs-to-release-more-release-more-tentpoles-1201118909/|archive-date=September 12, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref> Eventually, a number of spinoff projects took precedence, owing to the relative failure of ''The Amazing Spider-Man 2'', and sequel development collapsed when Sony entered a new licensing agreement with Marvel and parent company [[Walt Disney Studios (division)|Walt Disney Studios]].<ref name="VenomCarnage">{{Cite web |last=Busch |first=Anita |date=August 4, 2014 |title=Sony Launches Female Superhero Movie Mining Spider-Man Universe |url=https://deadline.com/2014/08/female-superhero-movie-spider-man-sony-810253/ |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170526155720/https://deadline.com/2014/08/female-superhero-movie-spider-man-sony-810253/ |archive-date=May 26, 2017 |access-date=May 20, 2017 |website=[[Deadline Hollywood]]}}</ref><ref name="VarietyASMSequelsSpinoffs">{{cite web|url=https://variety.com/2015/film/news/details-spider-man-appear-in-sony-and-marvel-movies-1201429039/|title=Spider-Man: How Sony, Marvel Will Benefit from Unique Deal (Exclusive)|work=[[Variety (magazine)|Variety]]|last=Lang|first=Brent|date=February 10, 2015|access-date=August 29, 2025|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20250829040031/https://variety.com/2015/film/news/details-spider-man-appear-in-sony-and-marvel-movies-1201429039/|archive-date=August 29, 2025|url-status=live}}</ref>

